{'answer': "Federated learning (FL) differs from local models in that FL enables multiple clients to collaboratively train a shared model without exchanging raw data, while local models are trained independently on each client's dataset. In infant movement analysis, FL substantially improves classification performance compared with independently trained local models, achieving performance approaching that of centralized training. 

UA-FedAvg, an uncertainty-aware variant of FedAvg, incorporates predictive entropy derived from MC-Dropout into the federated aggregation process, adjusting client contributions according to their predictive uncertainty. Experimental results show that UA-FedAvg and its variant incorporating validation loss generally outperform conventional FedAvg across evaluated data-split configurations. UA-FedAvg also achieved lower median predictive uncertainty across the three data splits compared to FedAvg, indicating that incorporating predictive uncertainty into the aggregation process provides consistent benefits under certain data partitioning schemes.

URL: https://arxiv.org/pdf/2609.31463", 'status': 'success', 'error': None}

{'answer': 'CG-HAF fuses severity probabilities with lesion-count, confidence, and area by combining averaged holistic severity probabilities from independently trained classifiers with structured lesion-burden descriptors from an object detector. Specifically, it aggregates severity probabilities across three independently trained global branches to reduce dependence on a single training run. These global probabilities are then fused with count-derived grade indicators, normalized lesion counts, detection-confidence statistics, and lesion-area evidence into an interpretable 12-dimensional representation. A validation-fitted, balanced logistic-regression meta-classifier produces the final grade from this representation. 

The fusion process involves creating a 12-dimensional feature vector $ z_i $, which includes the aggregated global probabilities $ \bar{p}_{i,k} $, a one-hot count-derived severity category $ e(g(c_i)) $, normalized lesion count $ c_i / 65 $, mean detection confidence $ \text{meanconf}_i $, summed detection confidence $ \text{sumconf}_i / 10 $, and summed lesion area $ \text{sumarea}_i / 10^6 $. The final grade $ \hat{y}_i $ is determined by the arg max of the probability distribution conditioned on $ z_i $.

