{'answer': "Current workforce preparation strategies for AI-rich computing emphasize the importance of cognitive skills such as critical thinking, problem-solving, and creativity, while acknowledging the role of AI in assisting with these tasks. Participants noted that although AI can handle much of the problem-solving, humans still need to oversee AI outputs and evaluate their accuracy, which maintains the importance of critical thinking and working memory. There was a conflicting view on creativity, with some believing it will become more important in an AI-rich future, while others felt AI's ability to generate creative ideas may reduce the need for human creativity.

Future workforce preparation strategies, as suggested by participants, focus on strengthening AI-use training so students learn to use AI effectively and efficiently. There is a call to reduce overreliance on AI, encouraging individuals to first solve problems on their own, learn, and then use AI for assistance. This approach aims to retain and develop cognitive skills, ensuring that students understand the importance of these skills and focus on their development in an AI-rich environment. The importance of most cognitive skills is expected to decline, but critical thinking will remain essential. The shift in the importance of cognitive skills is expected to be influenced by the increasing integration of AI in daily workflows and the need for human oversight and creativity. 

